meta {
  name: List Audit Notes
  type: http
  seq: 10
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/deal-audits/deals/{{auditDealId}}/notes
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns a thread array", function () {
    expect(res.getBody()).to.be.an("array");
  });

  test("entries carry type, summary, when and who", function () {
    const thread = res.getBody();
    if (thread.length > 0) {
      expect(thread[0]).to.have.property("type");
      expect(thread[0]).to.have.property("summary");
      expect(thread[0]).to.have.property("occurredAt");
      expect(thread[0]).to.have.property("userName");
    }
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /deal-audits/deals/:dealId/notes — the audit thread (PAC-72)

  Free-text notes **and** workflow events in one list, newest first.

  - **Module:** `deal_audits`
  - **Permission:** `deal_audits:read`
  - **Data scope:** assignee **or** reviewer (see **Set Audit Assignment**).

  Mixing the two is the point: "Dana submitted this" and "called the client,
  no answer" read as one conversation rather than two parallel logs.

  ## Storage
  These are ordinary `activities` rows — `subjectType: "dealAudit"` with a
  `dealAuditId` ref — not a separate comments collection. The timeline
  vocabulary already existed and reusing it means one renderer, one permission
  story and one place to look.

  ## Types you will see
  | type | written by |
  |------|------------|
  | `note` | a person, via **Add Audit Note** |
  | `audit_assigned` | Set Audit Assignment |
  | `audit_submitted` | Submit Audit For Review |
  | `audit_approved` / `audit_changes_requested` / `audit_sent_back` | Review Audit |

  Only `note` is client-writable. The five workflow types are **system-emitted**
  and deliberately absent from `LOGGABLE_ACTIVITY_TYPES` — a client able to POST
  `audit_approved` could clear its own audit.

  `audit_resolved` is **not** here: it hangs off an individual checklist item,
  not off the audit as a whole.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  [
    {
      "id": "665f...d21",
      "type": "audit_submitted",
      "summary": "Audit submitted for review",
      "occurredAt": "2026-08-21T10:04:00.000Z",
      "userName": "Pat Producer"
    }
  ]
  ```

  Capped at 100 entries, newest first, and unpaginated by design — an audit
  thread that long is a conversation that should have been a phone call.

  ## Errors
  - `403 Forbidden` — module disabled or missing `deal_audits:read`.
  - `404 Not Found` — no audit for that deal, or out of reach.
}
